			<div class="content" id="post-content-2353683">
									

<blockquote>
<p>It makes Dr. Rights look like an absolutely horrible person.</p>
</blockquote>
<blockquote>
<p>Dr. Rights just doesn't love her Because. More specifically, Because we want the reader to feel sad about the fact that this mother doesn't love her daughter. Why? I dunno! Because depression!</p>
</blockquote>
<p>The merits of bringing in mental illnesses and portraying them in fiction can be debated endlessly, sure, and whether or not their inclusion in the story works or not. But I have to take issue with calling someone horrible for suffering through a mental illness they can't help, because it's something I hear all the time, both to me and others.</p>
<p>You're also presuming that because someone has post-partum depression, they could never take care of their child or indeed give them any sort of quality care, except that's what Rights is doing here. The fact that she keeps pushing on to give her child a good life shows she's probably more caring and loving than a lot of parents. If you think it comes off as bland or not portrayed in depth, that's fine. But what I got from this is &quot;she's horrible because she suffers from a mental illness so she clearly can't take care of her kid&quot;, which annoys me to no end.</p>

							</div>

								
					
