														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2353690">
									

<p>Double-posting wouldn't be the best, so to reply to Americium and you:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>When Ihpkmn said &quot;because depression&quot; I gathered that he meant &quot;because no better reason than the trope of depression in fiction and literature for no other reason than to have a sad character.&quot; Ihpkmn is saying that the fact Rights doesn't feel motherly love is not explored beyond &quot;she has depression&quot; with no context that legitimizes depression.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>This is fair enough, but it wasn't exactly made clear. Particularly this bit:</p>
<blockquote>
<p>Ophelia doesn't seem to have anything wrong with her</p>
</blockquote>
<p>As if Ophelia needed to have something wrong with her to legitimatize Rights' issues, because then her depression could be &quot;validated&quot;. I dunno, maybe that's not what he meant, but it struck a big issue with me.</p>
<blockquote>
<p>That implies she never got treatment of any form. I don't care what anyone says. She lives in a first-world country. She's part of a scientific organization. There is an established psychologist in-universe- Doctor Glass. She should've gotten help by now.</p>
</blockquote>
<p>As I've learned, getting help can either get you over depression really quickly, or be an agonizing crawl. I'm sure Rights must have gotten help, but again, it's not that easy to get over for many people. Even Glass isn't perfect.</p>
<p>Also, she's proven she can take care of her child fine for her to grow up pretty healthy; nothing's wrong with her, after all, and Rights has put in a lot more care than even loving parents do with their kids. There's no urgent reason to give up the kid, and whether that's shitty or not, it's how it works sometimes in reality and in fiction, a choice Soulless ran with.</p>

							</div>

								
					
