															</div>
				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ragazzo" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(508888); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=508888&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608045326" alt="Ragazzo" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=508888)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/ragazzo" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(508888); return false;" >Ragazzo</a></span> <span class="odate time_1439043876 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">08 Aug 2015 14:24</span>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-2353893">
									

<p>I think some people are assuming the entire 'problem' rights is having depression of some kind. It's pretty obviously not, at least to me. From what I'm reading it seems like this tale is meant to invoke the fear parents have of not instinctively loving their kids immediately and without faltering. Hell, it's not even just a fear, it's something that actually happens. The portrayal of Rights here is not cliched or loaded with tropes(and even if it was tropes aren't necessarily BAD, they're prevalent for a reason) in this shows her failing to meet the expectations of success everyone around her showers her with. it would have been easy to end the tale earlier then soulless did, but they let us have a positive ending of sorts, showing how rights stayed the course even if she might not feel all the things she is 'meant' to feel. It's humanizing a character, and showing them actually struggle, and fail to meet their expectations for themselves, never mind society. And at the end of it all we get to see that Rights has redefined her expectations for herself, or at least tried to, but still feels inadequate somehow. It's not melodrama just because no one is in mortal peril.</p>
<p>So I guess that was a lot of rambling. Basically I'm a big supporter of this tale. I urge detractors to give it another read.</p>

							</div>

								
